Your question is a little vage. What resources are you refering to?
To fill in some of the blanks I can offer this. Both VMS and NT can support
TCP/IP. VMS requires an add-on like Digital TCP-IP/ formerly UCX.
Both systems can communicate via socket interfaces and can converse with utilites
or
tools like DNS, bootp, smtp-mail, telnet, ftp, ping, talk and network printer
support.... To access file systems you need an addon for NFS. NFS is availble for
NT as well. The alternative is DECNET. DECNET is native to VMS, but not to NT.
DECNET can be setup on NT using pathworks 7.0a @ $200/client. It supports LAT,
CTERM as well which allows conectivity to Terminal servers and host systems using
a terminal emulator. Printer support is also availale. File system access is
supported via the FAL network object. You can access files on NT from VMS by
using standard file opens like NTNODE"username password"::file_name.ext; A
printer driver is also available. The others resources like Memory or cpu, Tape
or other may require special software not readily available.







