In article <67gs2h$c9n$1@usenet88.supernews.com>, "Andrew E. Barnett" <aebarnet@intrex.net> writes:
:I am interested in any suggestions or references concerning OpenVMS access
:control strategies, with insight on such things as performance impacts and
:difficulties in system administration.

  You have asked a rather open-ended question here.

  I will assume you have read through the OpenVMS security documentation.
  And in particular, I will assume that you have read through the appendex
  on operating in an NCSC Class C2 security environment.

  We would need to know what the local access control and local auditing
  requirements are, and as we would then tend to work to a solution from
  that basis.  We would also need to know how much disk space can be made
  available for auditing logs, how much processor performance is available,
  and how much specific auditing is required -- a rather obvious set of
  linkages exist between these site-specific factors.

  Local requirements might include NCSC Class B1 features such as mandatory
  access control, which would entail the use of the SEVMS package.

  Or local requirements might just those for user accountability, and basic
  audits on local core files and data structures.

  And even with all this information, you may (will) end up prototyping
  your expected configuration(s).

  [The above information -- and much more -- is typically included in a
  security review, and there are various organizations that offer this
  and similar security services.]

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
